          "case_name": "In re Z.F. & B.F.",
          "citation": "No. 1609, Sept. Term 2024 (Md. App. Ct. July 1, 2025)",
          "court": "Appellate Court of Maryland",
          "year": 2025,
          "date_decided": "2025-07-01",
          "holding": "The court held that in CINA cases a department is generally required under the ADA to make reasonable accommodations for parents with disabilities in providing reunification services, provided the parent makes the disability and needed accommodation known, and affirmed the award of custody and guardianship to the grandparents.",
          "relevance": "It examines the adequacy of front-end services arranged for a disabled parent.",
          "quote": "the Department is generally required under the ADA to make reasonable accommodations for parents with disabilities in rendering reunification efforts in CINA cases",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/11087687/in-re-zf-bf/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 10621099
        },
        {
          "case_name": "In re James G.",
          "citation": "178 Md. App. 543, 943 A.2d 53 (2008)",
          "court": "Court of Special Appeals of Maryland",
          "year": 2008,
          "date_decided": "2008-02-29",
          "holding": "The court held that the juvenile court erred in finding reasonable efforts where the department made only a single vocational referral to address the father's unemployment, because the department's services must address the actual impediments to reunification.",
          "relevance": "It measures the sufficiency of front-end, non-court services offered to avert deeper intervention.",
          "quote": "the obligation to render \"reasonable efforts\" rests on the Department, not the parent",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/1990179/in-re-james-g/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 1990179
        },
        {
          "case_name": "In re Shirley B.",
          "citation": "419 Md. 1, 18 A.3d 40 (2011)",
          "court": "Court of Appeals of Maryland",
          "year": 2011,
          "date_decided": "2011-04-25",
          "holding": "Affirming the change of four children's permanency plans from reunification to adoption, the Court held that the reasonable-efforts requirement is case-specific and must be measured against the services actually at the department's disposal, so efforts were reasonable where the department made and pursued referrals but funding-dependent services never became available to a developmentally disabled mother.",
          "relevance": "It assesses the adequacy of front-end and non-court services arranged to avert the deeper intervention that followed.",
          "quote": "We shall hold that the \"reasonable efforts\" requirement is case-specific, and must be considered in light of the services at the Department's disposal.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/2556516/in-re-shirley-b/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 2556516
        }
      ],
